Z||000021||FieroGT||10-07-1999||09:47 AM||||I think that there has been a lot of confusion over these "blue" headlights, lately. They are being mistaken for the "High Intensity Discharge" (HID) lights as seen on Lincoln MkVIII's, new BMW, Audi, and other premium brands. These "cool blue" lights and "Xenon" lights are NOT the same as the HID offered as OEM by the aforementioned marques. A true HID system is very complex and involves more than a bulb swap. The HID headlights require a large capacitor and transformer to turn them on. Trying to turn them on with conventional headlight wiring would take about 20 minutes. Just wanted to clarify this with some people who think that these "blue bulbs" are an upgrade to HID when they're not. I know how it feels to buy something expecting an upgrade and to be terribly disappointed after installation is complete. Just my $0.02. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Dave85||10-07-1999||12:49 PM||||The lights at Wal-Mart are GE H6054HO. They have a demo tape playing and they are supposed to put out a wider beam as well as brighter.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Racer 1||10-08-1999||01:37 AM||||My Autzone guy said the hyperwhites are  coming in tomorrow. Im getting some for my Metro they already have the H4 type at the zone.||||reg||14
Z||000024||Dale||10-08-1999||02:15 PM||||There are basicly two forms of the "blue lights" the blue ion bulb which uses an ion gas that burns in the blue spectrum (producing bright white) and the blue coated bulbs which filter out yellow tones and some red tones from the spectrum.  They wont really apear blue when on but a bright white instead.  I have these on my truck 90/130w.||||reg||1
